WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.383
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.080
ELIZABETH SWEENY: I'm going to
kind of set the stage for us,

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.850
and I will say, whether
our users are enterprise

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.580
clients or individual
consumers, we really want them

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:17.770
to have a great experience.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.467
It's kind of the core of
what we're searching for.

00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:22.550
And I actually had a really
interesting experience

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.010
this past week that I
think kind of sums up

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.620
why this is an important
experience for your users,

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.700
and I'd like to
recount that with you.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.420
So if I'm moving a little
slowly on stage today,

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.080
it's because I actually had
an emergency abdominal surgery

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.000
just a few days ago.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.860
But the great part
is I didn't have

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.300
to change my slides at all.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.920
I just have a way better story
to go with them, so worth it.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.680
So exactly a week ago,
almost to the hour,

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.410
I started having abdominal
pain, and I was like, huh, OK,

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.060
I want to look up
what's going on.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.740
Why does this hurt?

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.260
And I want to know
treatment options.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.220
So I type into search,
"abdominal pain that

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:04.280
could kill horses," and I
see a few different site

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.170
options pop up.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:11.410
I click on one of the
first ones, and I get this.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:13.820
This did not delight me.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.280
I was very frustrated with this
outcome at that point in time.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.630
And so I pop out of this
site, because I'm like, this,

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.110
I can't handle right now.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.950
I return to the search results,
and I click another option.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.260
I get something even worse.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.400
Is it loading?

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.840
Is it happening?

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.910
There's no feedback, right?

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:33.845
And I'm stuck just
really wanting

00:01:33.845 --> 00:01:35.720
to know why I feel like
I'm going to explode,

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.950
and I'm not getting
any information.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:41.000
So it finally starts to
load, thank goodness.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.310
But the content that
is rendered on the page

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.400
isn't the content that I want.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.360
What was happening was something
that's normally further

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.510
down on the page, the way that
the code was being shipped,

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.490
was being rendered first.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.050
And so I was actually
being shown risk factors

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.420
for abdominal issues instead
of treatment options.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.170
So I want to know if I need
to go to the emergency room

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.660
to not die, and
instead, I'm being

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.330
told that I'm most
likely, given my symptoms,

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.780
a 60-year-old male who lives
in a retirement community.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:15.950
Not good.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.260
But finally, the
right content loaded,

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.200
and it basically
told me that, yes,

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.940
I do need to seek medical care.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.220
And I go to click on the link.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.870
It doesn't respond.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.490
The site's not interactive yet.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:32.300
The main thread is too
busy, and so I'm stuck.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.710
So this whole
experience, just no.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.860
You never want to give
this for your users.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.320
This is not what they want.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.250
This is the opposite of what
you need them to experience,

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.830
and users notice it,
and they hate it.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.150
But that's a problem that's
way too common on the web.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.780
And there are a lot of things
that can torpedo your ability

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.045
to avoid this, and
so that's what I want

00:02:52.045 --> 00:02:53.170
to talk to you about today.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.230
I want to talk about the
impetus behind building

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.800
a really high quality experience
and the core principles that

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.590
define that quality and
how you can effectively

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.430
measure, optimize, and
monitor those core principles.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.640
So we know how
frustrating it is to have

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.400
a bad experience on the web,
as my experience just showed.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.240
But how universal
is this reality?

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.940
Let's put it in the context
of stress response factors.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.510
So this is actually a consumer
study done by Ericsson,

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:26.740
and they asked consumers
to rank stress factors.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.630
Waiting in line at
a retail store--

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:31.440
we know that we hate this,
and we want our latte,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.590
and we want it now.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.450
OK, great.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:37.120
Even worse-- watching
a melodramatic TV show.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.430
And I stole this joke, but
it's too good to pass up--

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.260
Netflix and not chill.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.610
Then you can be standing at
the edge of a virtual cliff

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:47.770
and watching a horror
movie-- these are all

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:49.000
stressful experiences.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.450
But what's worse than "Cujo"?

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.430
Mobile delays on a site.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:55.120
Right?

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.128
It's the worst.

00:03:56.128 --> 00:03:57.670
Well, no, the only
thing that's worse

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:58.700
is solving a math problem.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.658
We don't want to make
our users do that either,

00:04:00.658 --> 00:04:02.950
but that I think we all
understood intuitively.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.230
So I'm standing
here, because I want

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.270
to make sure that all
stakeholders fundamentally

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:11.710
understand the importance
of developers spending time

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:15.620
on investing in these core
principles of quality.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.760
This is not an exercise of
espousing best practices just

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:19.810
for the sake of it.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.180
It is about your bottom line.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.070
So just to give some
more context for that

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.680
through an example, Pinterest
revamped their mobile web

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.180
experience to focus
on performance

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.190
and saw tremendous results.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:32.770
Their mobile website is
now their top platform

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:35.890
for sign-ups, and similarly,
after implementing

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.560
and enforcing a really
aggressive but reasonable

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.890
performance budget,
Tinder was able to see

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:46.130
more swipes on the web now than
they do on their mobile app.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.853
So over and over again,
we see the same pattern.

00:04:48.853 --> 00:04:51.520
Those who know how to design and
implement a really high quality

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.010
experience on the web see
increased satisfaction,

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:56.900
more time spent on pages, and
ultimately, higher revenue.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.560
So we see that sweet spot
is around two seconds

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.350
with conversion rates dropping
with every millisecond

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.010
of slowdown after that.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.560
So it's important
to have an accurate

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.080
measurement as well, which
we'll talk about a bit later.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.510
Performance sites
are profitable sites.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.642
That's the moral of the story.

00:05:12.642 --> 00:05:13.600
But where do you start?

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.947
There's a lot going on.

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:16.780
You start with a business
goal, effectively,

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:18.405
because that's what
you're looking for.

00:05:18.405 --> 00:05:21.130
You don't want to just have a
great FCP for the sake of FCP,

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:22.900
as cool as that is.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.280
You want to make
sure that you're

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.290
addressing core business goals.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.170
So what are those
core principles that

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:30.522
allow you to accomplish that?

00:05:30.522 --> 00:05:32.980
The example I gave earlier is
actually a really complicated

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:35.980
one, because my trying
to actually engage

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:39.190
with web content that
wasn't loading properly

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:41.900
caused a slew of
user pain points.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.330
It wasn't just a single problem.

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:46.400
It wasn't just that it
wasn't loading quickly.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:48.770
It was that it
was loading wrong.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:50.890
And like I said, it's
difficult to define quality,

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:52.570
so let's take a stab at it.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.232
There's a lot of ways
to break it down,

00:05:54.232 --> 00:05:55.690
but there are a
few core principles

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:57.670
that are fairly
canonical and don't often

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:02.950
shift, so even in the
face of strong new fads.

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:05.570
So I'd like to go over
those underlying principles,

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.320
the first step
being performance.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.780
Speed and the user
perception of speed

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.500
is going to be at the
core of our metrics

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:15.650
and tooling discussion
in a few minutes.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.670
But it's one of the
most critical points

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:20.570
that you're going
to want to focus on.

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:22.930
But there's also things
like accessibility,

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.880
and we want your site to be
accessible for everybody.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.690
Why would you want to miss
out on potential consumers?

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.120
This means that the site's
content and functionality

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.470
can be operated by
literally anyone.

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:36.070
It's really altogether
too easy to assume

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.490
that people engage with content
the same way that we do.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:42.850
And while we tend to center
the discussion of accessibility

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:44.740
on people with
physical impairments,

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:48.010
often, we can all
relate to experiences

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:53.650
of using an interface in a way
that isn't particularly helpful

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:55.670
and that isn't accessible
for other reasons.

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:58.300
So for instance, if you
are using a desktop site

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.648
on a mobile phone, I
feel like I'm can just

00:07:00.648 --> 00:07:02.440
stop with the examples
there, and everybody

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:04.900
can feel the pain, or
seeing the message,

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:07.180
this content is not
available in your area, which

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:10.390
in India, could
not watch anything,

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:13.900
or been unable to find a
familiar menu item on a tablet.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:18.470
All these things are
accessibility issues as well.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:21.610
For discoverability, everything
else is kind of a moot point

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:23.620
if people don't find you.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:25.420
So there are a lot
of things that you

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:27.940
can do to help in terms of
how you actually implement

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:31.070
your site, such as
giving your site a really

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:33.260
strong URL structure.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.240
I mean the more structure
you give, the more

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.040
indexable you're going to be.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.680
Making your site responsive
and that it's easily consumable

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.850
on all devices, using
rel canonical and rel

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.532
alternative for separate
desktop and mobile sites--

00:07:45.532 --> 00:07:47.240
there are a slew of
other tips and tricks

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:48.740
to make your site
more discoverable.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:50.240
But at the end of
the day, we want--

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.130
the web is all about
driving people to you.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:54.860
That's one of the things we're
best that, so to leverage it

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:58.160
to the full extent of
its ability, smart move.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:01.040
Security-- I mean from
authenticating your users,

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:04.580
maintaining sessions with
them, encrypted communications,

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:07.040
security is kind of a
universally accepted core

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:08.630
quality.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:11.220
And it encompasses
so many subtopics,

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.220
but at the end of the
day, if you think about it

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:14.780
from the perspective
of the end user,

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:17.450
if they're not comfortable
interfacing with you

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:20.360
and giving you any sort
of tangible insight

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:25.460
into their needs, you're
both going to hurt.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:28.240
Smoothness-- I really,
really like coffee.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.750
And I love coffee.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:32.809
But smoothness on
a web page means

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:36.110
that things aren't
janky or moving around

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:39.330
everywhere just kind
of in a weird way.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:42.740
It means that animations
aren't stuttering or unnatural,

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:46.040
and it also means that
everything just feels seamless.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:48.560
And we all know what that
feels like, and it feels good.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:51.440
So fundamentally, this comes
down to a few core concepts--

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.813
choosing the right
things to animate

00:08:53.813 --> 00:08:55.730
and being aware of the
performance trade-offs,

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:58.430
because sometimes they can
be unexpectedly draconian.

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:00.650
So watching out for that is key.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:02.720
Using animations to
support interactions

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.660
and not unnecessarily
hindering the page

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:09.470
or the user's interactions--
so we all remember Clippy.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.490
Like, we-- yeah.

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:12.920
Enough said.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:15.860
And avoid animating
expensive properties.

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:18.950
Basically, stick to transforms
and opacity changes.

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:22.940
If all else fails, those
are your guiding lights.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:25.880
So all of these core
principles amount

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:29.780
to being able to answer this
question with a resounding

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:33.320
"heck yes," because you
want to let your users,

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:37.460
and delighting your users means
performant, accessible, secure,

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:40.213
discoverable, and smooth sites.

00:09:40.213 --> 00:09:42.380
And it's interesting to
consider the core principles

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:45.500
and how they've evolved in the
context of both native and web

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:46.580
applications.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:48.410
Many of the web
applications that we've

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:50.420
seen develop over
the past few years

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:53.030
have been to enable the
same richness of experience

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:55.100
that people have
become traditionally

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:57.230
accustomed to in native apps.

00:09:57.230 --> 00:09:59.180
And many of the web
technologies that Thomas

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:01.010
is going to talk
about that we've

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:03.930
seen develop over the past
few years, like I said,

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.030
are to do this.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.520
And the strength of the web
has been primarily in reach,

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:11.260
but these web technologies have
been changing that equation.

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:14.210
So yeah, I already
said this slide.

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:16.040
I am just like--

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:16.640
yeah.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.140
I'm going to keep going.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:20.810
That's one of the
reasons why PWAs

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:26.360
came about is to really
make that rich experience

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:30.080
achievable in a much more
simple, straightforward way.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:32.450
So progressive web apps
allow your web experience

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:35.258
to be fast because of
caching and prefetching,

00:10:35.258 --> 00:10:37.550
integrated, because it gives
you that prime real estate

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:38.960
on the home screen.

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:43.340
It's reliable with network
fluctuations because of service

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:46.370
workers and offline
capabilities,

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:48.690
and then engagement
with push notifications.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:50.330
So it feels very
intuitive for the user

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.240
to engage with it as they
would in a native application.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:57.530
And ultimately, the slew of
technologies that underpin PWAs

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:02.010
allow for easier achievement of
quality, as we've talked about.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:04.875
So as we saw before, Pinterest
updated their web experience

00:11:04.875 --> 00:11:06.000
and saw tremendous results.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.720
But this was with a PWA,
just to kind of fall

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:10.070
back on that context.

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:12.590
And they ultimately were
looking and analyzed usage

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:15.237
for unauthenticated
mobile web users,

00:11:15.237 --> 00:11:16.820
and they realized
that their old, kind

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:21.620
of out-of-date experience only
managed to convert 1% of users

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:24.540
into sign-ups, logins,
or native app installs.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:27.630
And the opportunity to improve
this conversion was huge.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.810
So things like
boosting engagement

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:32.590
with push notifications
was a big deal for them.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:36.770
And as a result, they
see time spent up 40%,

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.800
and core engagement is up 60%.

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:40.910
So what's the takeaway?

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:43.190
The core principles
foundationally

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:46.020
underlie the acquisition,
engagement, conversion,

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:47.460
and retention of your users.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.370
That's why they're canonical.

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:52.370
Performance, accessibility,
security, discoverability,

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:53.750
and smoothness--

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:56.720
they all directly impact
the business's ability

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:00.770
to execute against its
most rudimentary goals.

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:03.330
So if these principles
are so important,

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:06.590
then why do we regress or
not really ever kind of

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:08.580
get started with them?

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:11.150
Before we get into some of
the more nitty-gritty of how

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.730
we measure these core
principles quantitatively,

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.820
I wanted to take a look at
some of the components that

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:20.810
are less technical that cause
challenges to actually execute

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:22.860
against this high
quality experience.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:24.680
And one of the challenges is
having all the stakeholders

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:25.180
onboard.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.750
You want to have the
organization fully bought

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:33.880
in to the fact that we have a
stake in these core principles

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:36.400
and that it has a direct
effect on your bottom line.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:39.885
If there's any disjoint in
that, it causes problems.

00:12:39.885 --> 00:12:41.260
So let's take this
as an example.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:43.810
I know it's a busy
slide, and I apologize.

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:47.050
But in the red org structure,
performance and other core

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:49.425
implementations are
siloed from marketing

00:12:49.425 --> 00:12:50.800
and other critical
business teams

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:53.170
with no visibility,
shared accountability.

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:55.960
It's kind of difficult.
But even when it's shared,

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:58.330
stakeholders don't often
know who shares the code.

00:12:58.330 --> 00:12:59.800
So that makes it difficult.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.300
Things are better
in the green org

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.850
structure, where the core
principles of quality

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:05.280
are well-defined.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:08.290
They're owned by management,
and they're enforced throughout.

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:10.000
It needs to be one
of the first things

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:11.417
that a team considers
when they're

00:13:11.417 --> 00:13:13.420
designing something
new, not something to be

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:14.572
tacked on later.

00:13:14.572 --> 00:13:16.030
I think most people
in the audience

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:19.120
probably understand that
pain a little too acutely,

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:21.910
because there is nothing
more painful than having

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:24.430
to layer on security
or performance

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:28.600
onto a fundamentally
insecure non-performant site.

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:31.340
It is painful.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:35.350
But this can be seen in
how organizations often

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:38.320
approach the designing
of their web apps.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:40.270
Performance is an
afterthought, and a lot

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:42.430
of the core principles
come to light

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:45.790
in the heat of an emergency,
like customers are complaining.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:46.960
We're losing money.

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:48.250
Panic ensues.

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:51.820
And then you get Band-Aid
fixes, and as soon

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:57.820
as the fire tamps down, those
things go down in the priority.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:01.880
And that's not sustainable.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:03.950
But like I said, at
the end of the day,

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:06.940
these core principles are
about solving business problems

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:09.280
and understanding that
the conversation--

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:13.360
we need to increase conversions,
and we need to lower our FCP--

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:16.210
are the same
conversation is something

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:21.460
that is so critical to get
both business stakeholders

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:27.140
and engineering really excited
about solving these problems.

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:28.780
Now it's time to get
to the good stuff.

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:31.720
So we can't improve
what we can't measure.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:33.220
We need to know
what we're measuring

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:34.580
and how to measure it.

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:36.700
So let's start with a
brief history of things,

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:40.420
just to whet the appetite again
for why measuring precisely

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:41.950
has become so important.

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:45.040
So welcome to the
human attention span.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:48.910
Even in 2000-- yeah,
that was nine years ago,

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:52.630
I realized, a few days
ago, which is terrifying.

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:55.960
The average human could
have barely waited

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:57.970
for the average page
in the Netherlands

00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:59.800
to load- just
couldn't handle it.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:01.270
You'd walk away.

00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:03.640
Even a goldfish would
be too impatient to wait

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:07.030
for the average
EMEA site to load.

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:10.210
And then in 2013, we
got even more impatient,

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:15.530
and our attention decreased to
lower than that of a goldfish.

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:16.780
Humanity's great.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:19.600
And now here's the
good and the bad news.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:21.490
Online, you really
only have three seconds

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:23.278
before you lose people.

00:15:23.278 --> 00:15:25.570
And I actually don't know
why that's good and bad news.

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:28.847
It's just kind of news that
needs to be accounted for.

00:15:28.847 --> 00:15:30.430
So you have three
seconds, but there's

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:32.530
a lot to do in three
seconds, and it's always

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:36.580
a balancing act, and you're
going to have to prioritize.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:38.610
The impact on user
experience is critical.

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:42.610
So you look at the fact that
the speed it takes a load a page

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:45.250
is the most important
factor for a user,

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:48.310
far and above how easy it
is to find what they want,

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:50.770
more important than the
simplicity of using the site,

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:53.320
and three times more
important than how

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:55.780
attractive the site looks.

00:15:55.780 --> 00:15:57.940
So performance is important.

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:00.340
You're going to be really
bored of hearing me saying that

00:16:00.340 --> 00:16:02.450
by the end of this.

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.720
But before we learn
where to measure,

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:06.940
what are we actually measuring?

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:08.860
Loading isn't a
single moment in time,

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:11.823
and there isn't one metric
that can fully capture it.

00:16:11.823 --> 00:16:13.240
So this gets back
to my experience

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:16.240
that when a page loads,
it begs the question, what

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:18.330
is performance?

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:20.740
It's partly the speed of
initial load, to be sure,

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:22.540
but how do we actually
break that down?

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:24.220
And focusing on user
performance metrics

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:26.290
is a great way to get started.

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:28.610
A user perceives that
something is happening.

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:29.980
Am I receiving content?

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:32.560
That's-- is it happening?

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:35.440
And that's first paint and
first [INAUDIBLE] pain.

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:36.400
Is it useful?

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:39.400
Is what I'm receiving
visible and digestible?

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:40.990
That's first meaningful paint.

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:41.950
And then is it usable?

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:43.930
If I click on it,
will it respond?

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:46.180
That's time to interactive.

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:48.910
Taking one step
down, so FCP actually

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:51.310
measures the time
from navigation

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:54.940
to the time when a browser
first renders the first bit

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:56.450
of content onto the page.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:16:58.480
So it's an important
milestone for the user,

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:01.090
because it provides feedback
that something's actually

00:17:01.090 --> 00:17:03.760
happening and that
the page is loading.

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:07.359
The time to interactive metric
measures how long a page takes

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:10.210
to actually become
interactive, so it means

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:13.060
that interactive
defined by a page

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:15.280
has displayed useful content.

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:18.280
Event handlers are
registered, and the page

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:21.319
responds to user interactions
within 50 milliseconds.

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:23.440
So it's a difficult
metric to achieve,

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:25.640
and it's much later in
load, but it's important,

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:28.680
because you know
that rage clicking?

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:31.130
That's that.

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:34.510
And this is a great example of
a metric that's great in the lab

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:36.550
but doesn't work as
well in the field,

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:39.740
because people
actually have to--

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:41.950
did I swap that?

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:43.390
I don't know if I swapped that.

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:46.810
So yeah, time interactive,
great in the lab,

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:48.890
not so great in the field,
because in the field,

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:51.490
you need actually somebody
responding and pushing

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:55.150
on the screen to get that
input, whereas with TTI, it's

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:56.810
just simulated.

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:58.810
And we'll talk about the
differences between lab

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:00.100
and field in a minute.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:04.540
But FID is where we're
talking about-- it measures

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:09.410
the time from when a user first
interacts, clicks, or taps,

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:11.410
and when the browser is
actually able to respond

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:13.010
to that interaction.

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:16.150
So in general,
input delay happens,

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:18.290
because the browser's
main thread is too busy,

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:20.290
and we're doing something
else, and it can't yet

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:21.760
respond to the user.

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:23.620
We all know that.

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:25.180
One common reason
that can happen

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:28.890
is the browser is busy
parsing a lot of JavaScript.

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:30.940
We know that depending
on how you bundle it,

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:33.620
it'll affect this
experience for the user.

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:37.600
And to dive one step deeper than
that, on the goldfish slide,

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:38.980
you lose people
at three seconds,

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:43.790
but they notice latency at
anything over 100 milliseconds.

00:18:43.790 --> 00:18:45.350
So that's something
to keep in mind.

00:18:45.350 --> 00:18:48.460
We perceive-- we
perceive that change,

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:53.180
and that measurement is
incredibly important.

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:55.000
So quick raise of hands.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:58.470
Who prefers A?

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:00.650
Who prefers B?

00:19:00.650 --> 00:19:01.920
Yeah.

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:07.830
Unfortunately, some sites
optimize content visibility

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:09.690
at the expense of interactivity.

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:11.830
So you have A. It's great.

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:12.540
It's loaded.

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:13.810
But you're clicking on it.

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:17.760
It's not doing anything,
whereas with B, you're

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:20.670
not seeing something, but as
soon as you see something,

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:22.890
you click, and it works.

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:24.750
Both kind of suck.

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:25.440
Right?

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:28.992
Like, eh, you want
some healthy balance.

00:19:28.992 --> 00:19:30.450
To the best of your
ability, you're

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:34.960
trying to optimize for
both, and that's hard to do.

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:36.710
So why do we care about
all these metrics?

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:39.350
Because all of them represent
a different part of the user

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:42.480
experience, and as we said,
it's not a single metric.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.110
It's really a
cohesive experience.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:45.025
So you'll see--

00:19:45.025 --> 00:19:47.150
I know we have a lot of
moving goal posts sometimes

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:48.620
with metrics, but
we're constantly

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:51.740
trying to find what is
actually representative

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:54.865
of what the user is experiencing
when something loads.

00:19:54.865 --> 00:19:56.990
So there are two sides to
the measurement equation,

00:19:56.990 --> 00:19:58.738
as I kind of alluded to earlier.

00:19:58.738 --> 00:20:00.530
There's performance in
the lab, and there's

00:20:00.530 --> 00:20:02.070
performance in the real world.

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:03.945
Now you can look at this
from the perspective

00:20:03.945 --> 00:20:07.440
of both metrics, as well as
measurement of those metrics.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:10.490
So lab measurement,
synthetic testing,

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:11.990
where you can test code quality.

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:14.073
The others field where
you're looking at real user

00:20:14.073 --> 00:20:19.010
interactions and understanding
how they are actually

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:21.110
experiencing a site.

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:22.670
Some metrics are
available in both.

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:24.680
Some, one or the other.

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:27.110
Like I said, nearly everything
is available in the lab,

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:31.640
but FID is unique, because
you need that real user input.

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:35.960
And TTI and FID are
interactivity metrics,

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:39.516
so they're going to be sensitive
to the main thread activity.

00:20:39.516 --> 00:20:41.720
It's good to kind of
get a sense of when

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:44.240
you're going to get that rage
clicking scenario going on.

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:46.980
So lab data is fantastic
for a lot of reasons.

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:49.370
It gives a sense of how
changes are likely to behave

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:50.910
in the real world.

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:54.710
You can catch problems
before making things go live.

00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:57.050
They're helpful for
debugging performance issues,

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:58.130
reproducible.

00:20:58.130 --> 00:20:58.977
It's great.

00:20:58.977 --> 00:21:00.560
But the downsides
are that they're not

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:02.930
going to really capture
real world bottlenecks,

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:05.540
and they can't correlate
against real world KPIs.

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:08.100
So that's a downside.

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:10.340
So that's where
field data comes in,

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:12.050
and it complements
it beautifully.

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:13.850
Real world's
messy-- permutations

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:16.550
of devices, network
configurations,

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:17.960
cache conditions.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:19.520
I mean, it's
impossible to simulate

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:22.220
all these things in the lab,
and you need those real signals.

00:21:22.220 --> 00:21:23.750
So RUM helps--

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:25.370
Real User Measurement
actually helps

00:21:25.370 --> 00:21:27.950
you understand what
matters most to real users,

00:21:27.950 --> 00:21:30.080
their actual behavior,
and that might

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:33.820
be different than
your assumptions.

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:34.860
Best strategy?

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:36.100
Combine the two.

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:37.510
Having both lab
and field metrics

00:21:37.510 --> 00:21:39.940
is a proxy for what your
users are experiencing

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:43.040
and how they're experiencing the
core principles of your site.

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:44.980
So we know we want
lab and field data.

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:45.610
Great.

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:47.495
Where do we get it?

00:21:47.495 --> 00:21:49.120
So the primary three
metrics that we've

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:51.280
discussed kind of on
an ongoing basis today

00:21:51.280 --> 00:21:53.440
are available in their
respective lab and field

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:54.400
environments.

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:57.280
Because FCP can be measured
both in the lab and field

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.940
with users, it is
available across the board

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:02.560
in tools like Lighthouse,
PageSpeed Insights, Chrome User

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:06.820
Experience Report, and actually
as a web perf API as well.

00:22:06.820 --> 00:22:08.547
TTI is only
available in the lab,

00:22:08.547 --> 00:22:10.630
so it can be accessed via
Lighthouse and PageSpeed

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:14.080
Insights, and FID requires the
real user input, as we said,

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:17.470
so you can go to CrUX for that.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:19.930
My tip of the day, just
to make your life easier--

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:23.950
if you want to get a snapshot
of your site's lab and field

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:27.380
performance to benchmark
overall quality,

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:29.740
go run your page through
PageSpeed Insights.

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:32.740
So you can do this via a web
app at the URL shown here

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:36.160
or via the V5 API
that we launched

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:38.260
in November of last year.

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:39.820
At the top of the
PSI report, you'll

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:41.762
see a high level
performance score, which

00:22:41.762 --> 00:22:43.720
is the same that you see
if you run Lighthouse,

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:46.720
and it's calculated based
on the lab metrics that

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:48.740
are measured and weighted.

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:50.740
Metrics that contribute
to this high level score

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:55.447
include things like FCP,
FMP, Speed Index, and TTI.

00:22:55.447 --> 00:22:57.280
And you'll clearly see
in the field report--

00:22:57.280 --> 00:23:00.352
in the PSI report, that
you've run both field and lab,

00:23:00.352 --> 00:23:01.810
which is really
cool to have in one

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:07.150
place, and from their respective
sources, CrUX and Lighthouse.

00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:09.880
So the two primary field metrics
are pulled out for the URL

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:11.590
that you run.

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:15.610
And so for each metric, you see
the percentage of page loads

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:20.733
that, in the real world,
were slow, average, or fast.

00:23:20.733 --> 00:23:22.150
What a tool like
Lighthouse offers

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:24.700
you is that if you use it
via one of the surfaces

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:27.220
other than PSI, it
gives you a means

00:23:27.220 --> 00:23:29.440
to measure more of
the core principles

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:31.080
aside from just performance.

00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:34.000
So you can audit your site for
accessibility, best practices,

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:36.760
search engine optimization,
and which criteria

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:38.736
have been filled for PWA.

00:23:38.736 --> 00:23:41.680
Lighthouse also allows
you to dive more deeply

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:44.140
into opportunities
for each category,

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.000
making improving the
quality of your site

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:48.670
really actionable and tangible.

00:23:48.670 --> 00:23:51.550
So setting performance
budgets are a fantastic way

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:54.730
to keep from regressing as
well and can be one of the most

00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:56.290
effective ways to
monitor, so we're

00:23:56.290 --> 00:23:59.380
talking measuring,
optimizing, and monitoring.

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:01.230
This is where the
monitoring comes in.

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:04.030
Budgets can take on many forms,
but setting them and enforcing

00:24:04.030 --> 00:24:06.010
them, even in the
context of the latest

00:24:06.010 --> 00:24:07.480
and greatest new feature--

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:08.980
it's great to push
back on features

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:12.490
you don't want to implement,
by the way, pro tip--

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:15.580
it's going to help your
pocketbook big time.

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:17.590
So there are a
slew of tools that

00:24:17.590 --> 00:24:20.032
are at your disposal
that can help

00:24:20.032 --> 00:24:21.490
you gauge the
quality of your site,

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:23.620
ranging in level
of technicality.

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:25.930
And I encourage you to
enjoy playing with them,

00:24:25.930 --> 00:24:27.550
both in the lab and field.

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:30.710
Your users and your bottom
line will thank you for it.

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:34.670
And with that, I pass it to
my amazing colleague, Thomas.

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:36.700
THOMAS NATTESTAD: Thank
you so much, Elizabeth.

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:38.283
Yeah, round of
applause for Elizabeth.

00:24:38.283 --> 00:24:39.010
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:41.800
She literally dragged herself
out of the hospital bed

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:43.600
to be here in order
to give this talk,

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:47.590
and if that's not caring about
metrics, I don't know what is.

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:51.688
So for my part of the talk,
I want to share about some

00:24:51.688 --> 00:24:53.230
of the new things
that we're building

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:55.960
in Chrome that
fundamentally expand

00:24:55.960 --> 00:25:00.460
the set of experiences that are
possible to create on the web.

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:03.430
And in order to
showcase how impactful

00:25:03.430 --> 00:25:07.340
some of these improvements and
technological changes can be,

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:11.290
I want to first take a step
way back and understand the web

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:14.380
as a platform and as
an application platform

00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:19.570
compared to how Android,
iOS, and Windows are also

00:25:19.570 --> 00:25:21.700
these application platforms.

00:25:21.700 --> 00:25:23.590
And so to really
take a step back,

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:26.680
I'm going to step way back
to the first ever web page

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:28.960
published by Tim Berners Lee.

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:30.880
And as you can
see, this web page

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:34.330
is really just very slightly
formatted text, some of which

00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:36.730
you could click on to get
to other pages of slightly

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:37.510
formatted text.

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:39.490
And that's all the web was.

00:25:39.490 --> 00:25:42.070
Then a little something
called JavaScript came along,

00:25:42.070 --> 00:25:46.060
which was famously created and
written in a 10-day hackathon.

00:25:46.060 --> 00:25:48.010
And this 10-day hackathon
brought JavaScript

00:25:48.010 --> 00:25:52.180
and brought interactivity to
the web for the first time ever.

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:54.310
Suddenly, you could
respond to user input,

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:57.280
do dynamic logic on
your page itself,

00:25:57.280 --> 00:25:59.620
and even with this
very basic set

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:01.660
of interactivity
functionality, you

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:04.417
saw the first wave
of web applications.

00:26:04.417 --> 00:26:07.000
In the past, things like Outlook
and other native applications

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.600
had been the only
way to do email,

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:10.600
and now suddenly, you
could just type Gmail.com,

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:13.150
and you were in an
email client, where

00:26:13.150 --> 00:26:15.700
you could send and receive
and manage all your emails.

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:18.250
And there were, of course,
many other applications that

00:26:18.250 --> 00:26:21.160
followed along, things
like MapQuest or eBay,

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:24.040
things that were highly
interactive and dynamic now

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:27.820
being able to be delivered
directly through the browser.

00:26:27.820 --> 00:26:32.050
But why were these applications
actually moving to the web?

00:26:32.050 --> 00:26:34.440
In the native world, you can
use any language you want,

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:35.430
not just JavaScript.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:36.900
You have full
access to everything

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:38.610
that the computer can do.

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:41.070
You can do absolutely anything
to your heart's desire,

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:44.465
so why were applications
actually moving to the web?

00:26:44.465 --> 00:26:45.840
Well, it turns
out that there are

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:48.000
three fundamental
properties that

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:50.758
make the web a really
unique platform.

00:26:50.758 --> 00:26:52.800
The first of these, and
one that is unsurprising,

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:56.820
is linkability, the ability to
have a single string that then

00:26:56.820 --> 00:27:00.240
maps to the web page itself, and
have that string be clickable

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:02.760
from any other page so that
you can find your application

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:04.620
really easily,
but also shareable

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:06.660
so that I can share
a simple string that

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:09.090
shares not only the
application itself, but also

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:11.490
a specific document
for that application

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:14.550
that can now be
instantly accessed.

00:27:14.550 --> 00:27:17.035
The second property of the
web is its ephemerality.

00:27:17.035 --> 00:27:19.410
And this Is a very fancy
word that basically

00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:22.380
means that the user doesn't
have to worry about installing

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:25.500
the application, do they have
enough space on their machine,

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:27.600
and do they actually
trust the application.

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:29.370
The application or
website is delivered

00:27:29.370 --> 00:27:31.590
through the browser
completely sandboxed,

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:33.660
and the user never has
to worry about clicking

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:35.550
a link the same way they
should be absolutely

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:39.660
terrified about
installing an EXE.

00:27:39.660 --> 00:27:42.930
And lastly, the web is the
only truly universal platform,

00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:45.510
meaning that your application
is accessible by anyone

00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:46.740
on any device.

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:49.650
Today, the web obviously
runs on desktop, laptops,

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:52.050
and mobile phones,
but also on watches,

00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:54.810
and I've seen the web
literally running on treadmills

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:56.070
and refrigerators.

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:58.070
And this means that you
can use your single code

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:00.360
base to power all
of your deployments,

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:04.050
even to reach your
fridge-based users.

00:28:04.050 --> 00:28:05.790
So now, hopefully,
that we all have

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:08.520
a better shared understanding
of what the web is and some

00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:11.250
of the properties that define
it, I want to share about some

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:13.185
of the new technologies
that we're building in

00:28:13.185 --> 00:28:16.020
to Chrome that developers
can use to, again,

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:17.850
expand the set of
things that are actually

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:20.080
possible to do on the web.

00:28:20.080 --> 00:28:22.080
And the first of these
that I want to talk about

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:23.760
is WebAssembly.

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:26.860
WebAssembly is a new
low level binary format,

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:30.240
which means that it's close to
the CPU virtual machine code

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:33.870
that your computer understands,
but with all of the safety

00:28:33.870 --> 00:28:36.810
and sandboxing that you've
come to expect from the web.

00:28:36.810 --> 00:28:39.780
WebAssembly, much like
Assembly, is compiled from other

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:42.810
languages like C++, meaning
that you probably shouldn't be

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:46.440
writing WebAssembly by hand,
unless that's your thing.

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:49.590
And because WebAssembly is
a low level binary format,

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:53.190
it offers better maximized
and reliable performance

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:55.200
than JavaScript can.

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:58.770
So let's dive in to some of
the advantages of WebAssembly

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:00.370
in detail.

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:03.420
As I mentioned, WebAssembly
is compiled ahead of time from

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:06.570
something like C++, and that
means that you can do a lot

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:10.980
of optimizations and performance
tuning before the code actually

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:12.150
hits the browser.

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:15.090
JavaScript, by contrast,
is sent as raw source code

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:16.680
to the browser, and
then the browser

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:18.360
has to optimize it
as it's actually

00:29:18.360 --> 00:29:20.020
being executed on the page.

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:22.050
And this can cause
some of that choppiness

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:24.420
that you sometimes
get on web pages.

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:26.340
Critically, WebAssembly
also gives you

00:29:26.340 --> 00:29:28.480
more access to your CPU.

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:31.200
We recently launched
WebAssembly Threads,

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:34.230
and WebAssembly Threads let
you use the multiple cores that

00:29:34.230 --> 00:29:38.010
come on practically every
CPU to fully squeeze out

00:29:38.010 --> 00:29:40.080
every ounce of performance.

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:43.050
And to showcase some of
these performance differences

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:45.390
and to actually hear some
of these differences,

00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:47.400
I'm going to jump ahead
to the case studies

00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:50.520
that I'll get to later, and show
off one specific example called

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:51.600
Soundation.

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:53.730
Soundation is a fantastic
web application.

00:29:53.730 --> 00:29:57.810
They have a C++ code base, and
Soundation is a music editing

00:29:57.810 --> 00:30:00.420
studio similar to GarageBand
that lets you easily mix

00:30:00.420 --> 00:30:02.910
and match songs with
various effects.

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:06.360
They have a C++ application that
they compiled to WebAssembly,

00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:07.940
and it works really fantastic.

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:10.380
But sometimes when you have
to render really complex songs

00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:13.740
in real time, the performance
demands on that application

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:16.190
can be intense.

00:30:16.190 --> 00:30:18.660
We actually partnered with
them to try and render

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:20.730
one of their difficult
to render songs,

00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:23.040
and we tried to do this on
a single-threaded version

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:24.372
of their application.

00:30:24.372 --> 00:30:26.580
And we're going to get to
hear what that sounds like.

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:29.950
Fair warning-- this is not going
to be a pleasant experience.

00:30:29.950 --> 00:30:33.540
But if we have audio, which
it doesn't sound like we do--

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:34.486
[BROKEN MUSIC PLAYING]

00:30:34.486 --> 00:30:36.170
Ah, there it is.

00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:39.200
So I'm going to move past
that, because clearly,

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:41.780
we can all tell that is a
terrible experience, not

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:43.840
something you want your
users listening to.

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:46.490
But as soon as they turned
on WebAssembly Threads,

00:30:46.490 --> 00:30:48.545
they were able to
render that same song--

00:30:48.545 --> 00:30:49.355
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:30:49.355 --> 00:30:54.350
--now sounding much more normal
and using far less of the CPU.

00:30:54.350 --> 00:30:57.200
So that was just one performance
advantage of WebAssembly,

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:59.360
and letting you really
maximize everything

00:30:59.360 --> 00:31:01.880
that you can get out of the CPU.

00:31:01.880 --> 00:31:04.970
Another advantage of
WebAssembly is its portability.

00:31:04.970 --> 00:31:08.840
Because you compile WebAssembly
from other languages like C++,

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:11.180
you can port not only
your own applications that

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:15.350
you've written in C++, but also
the wealth of open source C++

00:31:15.350 --> 00:31:19.230
libraries and application that
exist out there in the world.

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:22.670
And because C++ specifically
is supported across practically

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:25.250
every platform, including
iOS and Android,

00:31:25.250 --> 00:31:28.970
you can now use C++ as your
common language across all

00:31:28.970 --> 00:31:31.610
of your different deployments,
even if you want that iOS

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:34.310
and Android native application.

00:31:34.310 --> 00:31:35.960
Lastly, and potentially
most exciting

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:38.280
to many of the developers
out there in the audience,

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:40.100
is the promise of
more flexibility

00:31:40.100 --> 00:31:41.600
when writing for the web.

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:43.850
In the past, JavaScript
was the only option

00:31:43.850 --> 00:31:46.970
that you had for fully
browser-supported language,

00:31:46.970 --> 00:31:49.330
and now with WebAssembly,
you get more choice.

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:52.670
The set of fully supported
languages today are C, C++,

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:55.050
and Rust, but there are
many other languages,

00:31:55.050 --> 00:31:58.250
including Kotlin and .NET,
which are working on adding

00:31:58.250 --> 00:32:00.073
experimental support
to WebAssembly,

00:32:00.073 --> 00:32:02.240
and some of these have
actually already shipped that

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:04.600
experimental support.

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:06.350
The most exciting thing
about WebAssembly,

00:32:06.350 --> 00:32:09.110
though, is that it is
fully launched in all four

00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:11.240
major browsers, meaning
that you can truly

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:15.467
use WebAssembly to reach every
single one of your web users.

00:32:15.467 --> 00:32:18.050
It's important to know, though,
at this point that WebAssembly

00:32:18.050 --> 00:32:21.410
is explicitly not a
replacement for JavaScript.

00:32:21.410 --> 00:32:24.710
Rather, WebAssembly is meant
to augment all of the things

00:32:24.710 --> 00:32:27.190
that JavaScript isn't
really designed for.

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:30.440
JavaScript is a dynamic
language, useful for scripting.

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:32.540
If you want to do
simple interactivity,

00:32:32.540 --> 00:32:35.510
JavaScript is and remains
the way to do that.

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:37.880
But if you want to really
maximize your performance,

00:32:37.880 --> 00:32:40.220
really get that
reliability, WebAssembly

00:32:40.220 --> 00:32:42.377
gives you that option.

00:32:42.377 --> 00:32:44.210
If you want to get
started with WebAssembly,

00:32:44.210 --> 00:32:46.700
the first thing to
check out is Emscripten.

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:50.030
Emscripten is a highly advanced
tool chain that not only helps

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:53.480
you compile your C++ into
the WebAssembly format,

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:55.910
but it also does a
lot of emulation.

00:32:55.910 --> 00:33:00.030
For example, if your application
uses open GL rendering calls,

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:03.380
Emscripten will automatically
transfer those open GL calls

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:05.900
into web GL calls so
that you can really

00:33:05.900 --> 00:33:09.140
easily wholesale port your
entire application using

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:11.690
the Emscripten tool chain.

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:13.710
OK, so that was
WebAssembly, and now I

00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:16.280
want to switch tracks
and talk a little bit

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:17.810
about some of the
new capabilities

00:33:17.810 --> 00:33:19.970
that we're adding to Chrome.

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:22.320
Again, going back to the
world's first ever web page,

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:24.320
we can see that you really
couldn't do anything.

00:33:24.320 --> 00:33:26.000
Outside of the
lack of JavaScript,

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:27.860
there really wasn't
any additional access

00:33:27.860 --> 00:33:31.370
or functionality that you
could get out of your web page.

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:34.022
Then the image tag came
along, and suddenly, you

00:33:34.022 --> 00:33:35.480
could show images
on your web page.

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:37.522
And that was a huge deal
and a major development.

00:33:37.522 --> 00:33:39.090
It was great.

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:40.940
But obviously, the
story didn't stop there.

00:33:40.940 --> 00:33:42.770
And today, many
of you likely know

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:46.330
that you can access location
and even get a camera feed.

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:48.080
You can take your
website into full screen

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:50.060
mode, all of these
different capabilities

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:51.860
that you've all seen.

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:54.260
But the picture is actually
so much more complex

00:33:54.260 --> 00:33:55.820
than most people imagine.

00:33:55.820 --> 00:33:58.520
Today, on the web, you can
do so much more, things

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:01.670
like accessing USB devices,
sending push messages,

00:34:01.670 --> 00:34:04.417
managing your credentials,
weaving files, syncing

00:34:04.417 --> 00:34:06.500
in the background, detecting
your device position,

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:07.520
vibration.

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:10.969
All of these different things
are accessible through browser

00:34:10.969 --> 00:34:13.400
standardized APIs.

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:15.790
But the story is
far from complete,

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:18.560
and I'm happy to say
that in 2019 and 2020,

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:20.780
Chrome is planning to
dramatically expand

00:34:20.780 --> 00:34:23.989
the set of capabilities that
your browser can access.

00:34:23.989 --> 00:34:27.020
These include things
like SMS verification,

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:28.850
the ability to see
the contacts that

00:34:28.850 --> 00:34:31.550
are on a device, task
scheduling, and even

00:34:31.550 --> 00:34:34.429
the ability to write back to
files that have been shared

00:34:34.429 --> 00:34:36.050
with your website
so that you can

00:34:36.050 --> 00:34:42.590
create a rich native-like
file editor or even an IDE.

00:34:42.590 --> 00:34:45.290
I want to dive into a few of
these specific capabilities

00:34:45.290 --> 00:34:46.739
in more detail.

00:34:46.739 --> 00:34:48.534
The first of these is offline.

00:34:48.534 --> 00:34:49.909
As you all know,
the web is great

00:34:49.909 --> 00:34:52.610
when you have a secure, reliable
connection, as most of us

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:56.043
do on desktop machines,
but not so much on mobile.

00:34:56.043 --> 00:34:58.460
The way that a traditional web
server works with a browser

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:00.043
is you go, and you
fetch your content,

00:35:00.043 --> 00:35:02.730
you load it into the browser,
and yeah, you're done.

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:05.420
But when you have poor
connectivity or even

00:35:05.420 --> 00:35:07.988
no connectivity, that's a
really negative experience,

00:35:07.988 --> 00:35:09.530
and you end up seeing
what's called--

00:35:09.530 --> 00:35:12.770
what we like to call the
offline-asaurus-rex or Offline

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:13.610
Dino.

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:16.190
And as much fun as this
game is-- which by the way,

00:35:16.190 --> 00:35:18.650
hit space, if any of
you haven't before--

00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:22.550
this isn't really the content
that users are looking for.

00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:24.200
Luckily, though, in
the last few years,

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:26.530
we've been launching something
called Service Worker.

00:35:26.530 --> 00:35:28.010
And what Service
Worker lets you do

00:35:28.010 --> 00:35:30.800
is that once you've actually
fetched your web application,

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:33.080
you can intelligently
cache it, and then

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:36.280
detect that when the user tries
to come back to your website,

00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:38.890
if they're on poor connectivity
or no connectivity,

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:42.340
you can serve that same web
page directly from the cache.

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:45.280
And then if the user eventually
enters a better connectivity

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:47.770
state, you can then
fetch the updated content

00:35:47.770 --> 00:35:51.430
and serve that to your
users on return visits.

00:35:51.430 --> 00:35:54.100
Another important capability
that we give you access to,

00:35:54.100 --> 00:35:56.740
if you have a service
worker in your application,

00:35:56.740 --> 00:36:00.550
is the ability to add your
website to the home screen.

00:36:00.550 --> 00:36:03.490
And this is what we call PWAs.

00:36:03.490 --> 00:36:06.520
And this is that ability to
render a button that says,

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:08.440
Install, and when
the user clicks it,

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:11.200
they'll say, yes, I want to
add this to my home screen.

00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:13.840
And then you actually
get a standalone icon

00:36:13.840 --> 00:36:15.255
on the user's device.

00:36:15.255 --> 00:36:16.630
And when they
click that icon, it

00:36:16.630 --> 00:36:20.260
opens up into a full screen
view with no URL bar at the top

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:22.660
whatsoever, and it
really feels and looks

00:36:22.660 --> 00:36:25.550
like a native application.

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:27.400
Another important
capability is the ability

00:36:27.400 --> 00:36:28.900
to send push messages.

00:36:28.900 --> 00:36:31.390
This is something that iOS
and Android has had access to

00:36:31.390 --> 00:36:33.400
since the beginning, but
it's also now something

00:36:33.400 --> 00:36:35.390
that you have access
to through the browser

00:36:35.390 --> 00:36:38.170
and have had access to
for the last few years.

00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:40.565
It works exactly the same way
it does in iOS and Android.

00:36:40.565 --> 00:36:42.190
And once the user
grants the permission

00:36:42.190 --> 00:36:44.110
to be shown push
notifications, you

00:36:44.110 --> 00:36:47.690
can send them directly
from your web application.

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:50.290
So these together kind of form
what we call Progressive Web

00:36:50.290 --> 00:36:52.260
Applications, or PWAs.

00:36:52.260 --> 00:36:53.680
And these are all
about delivering

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:56.800
that native look and feel
of an application using

00:36:56.800 --> 00:36:59.020
all of the web-based
technologies that are also

00:36:59.020 --> 00:37:02.930
so powerful and in the browser.

00:37:02.930 --> 00:37:05.680
And we're at a place today,
where you can quite literally

00:37:05.680 --> 00:37:08.620
create a PWA and a
native app experience

00:37:08.620 --> 00:37:11.140
alongside each other,
completely undetectable

00:37:11.140 --> 00:37:13.060
which one is the web experience.

00:37:13.060 --> 00:37:15.850
And you're welcome to try
and guess which of these two

00:37:15.850 --> 00:37:19.042
is the native and
which one is the PWA.

00:37:19.042 --> 00:37:21.250
For those of you who guessed
that the one on the left

00:37:21.250 --> 00:37:23.085
there is the PWA,
you were right,

00:37:23.085 --> 00:37:25.210
but I'm willing to guess
that that was mostly based

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:27.880
on random luck, because
these two experiences really

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:32.380
are interchangeable and
completely indistinguishable.

00:37:32.380 --> 00:37:35.680
I'm also happy to say that with
progressive web applications,

00:37:35.680 --> 00:37:38.200
we have now completely
expanded to desktop.

00:37:38.200 --> 00:37:40.365
It works exactly the
same way on desktop.

00:37:40.365 --> 00:37:41.740
You can render an
Install button.

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:43.340
When the user
decides to install,

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:45.580
you can simply say
yes, and then you

00:37:45.580 --> 00:37:48.400
get, again, that same
icon in the shelf,

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:50.530
as well as your own
standalone window,

00:37:50.530 --> 00:37:53.260
fully integrated into the
operating system, feeling

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:56.080
exactly like a
native application.

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:58.330
We also launched recently
something called Trusted Web

00:37:58.330 --> 00:37:59.500
Activities.

00:37:59.500 --> 00:38:04.120
Trusted Web Activities, or
TWAs, let you take your PWA

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:07.750
and turn it into an
Android native APK

00:38:07.750 --> 00:38:10.930
that you can list directly
in the Play Store.

00:38:10.930 --> 00:38:13.510
TWAs, instead of
using web views,

00:38:13.510 --> 00:38:16.420
use something called
Chrome Custom Tabs.

00:38:16.420 --> 00:38:21.430
And this means that TWAs share
and sync storage with Chrome.

00:38:21.430 --> 00:38:22.900
This means that if
a user is locked

00:38:22.900 --> 00:38:26.290
into your site on Chrome,
and they then go and download

00:38:26.290 --> 00:38:29.710
your TWA from the
Android Play Store,

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:32.200
they'll automatically be
logged in, and all of the data

00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:33.970
will be synced,
enabling them to switch

00:38:33.970 --> 00:38:35.620
between your
in-browser experience

00:38:35.620 --> 00:38:39.220
and your app experience
completely seamlessly.

00:38:39.220 --> 00:38:42.910
The resulting APK that you get
out on the other end for a TWA

00:38:42.910 --> 00:38:46.270
is also incredibly tiny, smaller
than any native application

00:38:46.270 --> 00:38:47.463
that you could have made.

00:38:47.463 --> 00:38:48.880
And this has been
really impactful

00:38:48.880 --> 00:38:51.700
for a lot of our partners
in emerging markets

00:38:51.700 --> 00:38:56.493
who struggle with storage
space on users' devices.

00:38:56.493 --> 00:38:58.410
All right, so those are
some of the highlights

00:38:58.410 --> 00:38:59.700
of some of the
different technologies

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:02.158
that we've been working on over
the last few years and some

00:39:02.158 --> 00:39:03.780
of the things yet still to come.

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:05.280
For the last part
of my talk, I want

00:39:05.280 --> 00:39:07.322
to quickly run through a
couple of different case

00:39:07.322 --> 00:39:09.530
studies of partners who
have used and adopted

00:39:09.530 --> 00:39:11.780
some of these new technologies
and some of the results

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:13.770
that they've found.

00:39:13.770 --> 00:39:17.040
The first of these that I
want to mention is Twitter.

00:39:17.040 --> 00:39:21.037
Twitter adopted the Add to Home
Screen and PWA functionality,

00:39:21.037 --> 00:39:22.620
and through that,
they found that they

00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:25.710
were able to completely
reinvigorate their mobile web

00:39:25.710 --> 00:39:26.760
experience.

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:29.280
They see as much as 1
million daily visits

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:31.620
of users who access their
application through that home

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:36.420
screen icon, as well as a
75% increase and a 65%--

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:40.440
a 75% increase in tweets
and a 65% increase

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:42.870
in page views/session
duration, which

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:44.880
is really powerful
for them and helps

00:39:44.880 --> 00:39:48.208
unlock more value
on the mobile web.

00:39:48.208 --> 00:39:50.250
Another interesting
application that we partnered

00:39:50.250 --> 00:39:52.750
with more recently is Spotify.

00:39:52.750 --> 00:39:56.290
Spotify was looking to solve
two fundamental challenges.

00:39:56.290 --> 00:39:58.440
First of all, they wanted
to lower the friction

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:00.430
of accessing their application.

00:40:00.430 --> 00:40:03.540
It's a very high ask to ask
your users to go and download

00:40:03.540 --> 00:40:06.330
an entire application
just to listen to one song

00:40:06.330 --> 00:40:09.360
that some friend of
yours sent you a link to.

00:40:09.360 --> 00:40:11.520
They also wanted to
solve storage space,

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:14.550
because it's hard for users
who don't know about Spotify,

00:40:14.550 --> 00:40:17.318
the application, to decide it's
worth the amount of storage

00:40:17.318 --> 00:40:19.110
on their device to
actually go and download

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:21.720
the native application.

00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:24.630
Spotify's approach was
to start small and use

00:40:24.630 --> 00:40:27.600
rigorous A/B testing to
detect the changes in user

00:40:27.600 --> 00:40:30.570
behavior resulting from
users who were shown the PWA

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:34.110
experience as opposed to being
redirected to the Play Store.

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:37.320
They found great results with
a 54% increase in day one

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:42.250
plays activations, a 14%
increase in retention,

00:40:42.250 --> 00:40:45.330
and as much as a 30%
reactivation of users

00:40:45.330 --> 00:40:49.140
who had churned and previously
uninstalled the app.

00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:52.020
Next up is Starbucks.

00:40:52.020 --> 00:40:54.360
Starbucks, similarly,
was trying to solve

00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:56.010
two fundamental challenges.

00:40:56.010 --> 00:40:58.980
First, they really wanted
their mobile web experience

00:40:58.980 --> 00:41:02.610
to have full UX parity with
their native application,

00:41:02.610 --> 00:41:05.670
and similar to Spotify,
they wanted a solution

00:41:05.670 --> 00:41:07.350
for users who didn't
have enough storage

00:41:07.350 --> 00:41:11.023
on their device for the
full native application.

00:41:11.023 --> 00:41:12.690
Their approach was
to start with as many

00:41:12.690 --> 00:41:15.210
of the performance basics
that Elizabeth did a fantastic

00:41:15.210 --> 00:41:17.460
job of laying out in
her section of the talk,

00:41:17.460 --> 00:41:20.700
focusing on less code,
intelligent caching, and image

00:41:20.700 --> 00:41:24.820
optimizations, as well as
adopting many of the PWA

00:41:24.820 --> 00:41:28.950
engaging and delightful
patterns to Add to Home Screen

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:32.430
and enable better sign-on
and authentication.

00:41:32.430 --> 00:41:35.410
Their results surprised
even us and themselves.

00:41:35.410 --> 00:41:37.860
They found that they
were able to double

00:41:37.860 --> 00:41:41.700
the amount of the time to
interactive for their web page,

00:41:41.700 --> 00:41:45.000
as well as a 65% increase
in Starbucks rewards

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:47.370
registrations, which is their
most important conversion

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:48.540
point.

00:41:48.540 --> 00:41:50.460
The last case study
that I want to go over

00:41:50.460 --> 00:41:52.410
is a really great company
that pulls together

00:41:52.410 --> 00:41:55.260
a lot of these principles,
and that's Figma.

00:41:55.260 --> 00:41:58.890
Figma is a powerful cloud-based
screen design platform

00:41:58.890 --> 00:42:02.910
that makes it really easy for
teams to work and collaborate

00:42:02.910 --> 00:42:05.370
in sync.

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:07.620
With native design
tools, you had

00:42:07.620 --> 00:42:09.780
to worry about managing
the right files

00:42:09.780 --> 00:42:12.182
and making sure that everybody's
files were up to date,

00:42:12.182 --> 00:42:14.640
making sure that everyone had
the right software installed,

00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:17.160
and whether or not
the operating system

00:42:17.160 --> 00:42:18.540
that one of your
team members was

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:22.270
using even supported the
application in the first place.

00:42:22.270 --> 00:42:24.540
Now by leveraging
the powers of links

00:42:24.540 --> 00:42:26.280
as the single
source of truth, you

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:28.620
can easily collaborate,
ping somebody

00:42:28.620 --> 00:42:32.160
a single string that they can
then click to instantly open up

00:42:32.160 --> 00:42:35.495
the application, jump right
in, collaboratively edit

00:42:35.495 --> 00:42:36.870
without having to
worry about any

00:42:36.870 --> 00:42:41.400
of the process of installing
the application itself.

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:44.040
I want to briefly mention the
actual setup that Figma has,

00:42:44.040 --> 00:42:46.560
because it's quite a well
done architecture that they

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:48.240
were able to come up with.

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:51.000
In the surrounding areas of
their application, the menus

00:42:51.000 --> 00:42:54.730
and such, they use a TypeScript
and React-based framework,

00:42:54.730 --> 00:42:56.010
which works really well.

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:58.200
And then inside of the
central editor section

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:01.138
of their application, they
have a single canvas tag,

00:43:01.138 --> 00:43:03.180
where they have a custom
rendering engine written

00:43:03.180 --> 00:43:07.470
in WebAssembly through C++ that
uses WebGL to rapidly render

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:11.430
that file and any changes
to that canvas area.

00:43:11.430 --> 00:43:13.500
They also have a
custom text editor,

00:43:13.500 --> 00:43:16.920
where they were able to take
the popular open source C++

00:43:16.920 --> 00:43:20.580
libraries, FreeType
and HarfBuzz,

00:43:20.580 --> 00:43:22.020
and compile those
to WebAssembly,

00:43:22.020 --> 00:43:23.970
and then just use
those directly-- again,

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:26.220
one of those portability
advantages that I was talking

00:43:26.220 --> 00:43:27.400
about earlier.

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:29.220
And then lastly,
they use web sockets

00:43:29.220 --> 00:43:33.960
to enable really fast and smooth
multiplayer editing of any file

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:36.870
in the Figma application.

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:39.180
Here are some of the links
to many of the resources

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:40.050
that I talked about.

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:41.550
I will give everyone
in the audience

00:43:41.550 --> 00:43:43.740
a second to take a
picture, or give everyone

00:43:43.740 --> 00:43:47.840
listening and watching at home
a chance to pause the video.

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:50.670
This is just some of the
useful links and resources

00:43:50.670 --> 00:43:52.080
that you have access to.

00:43:52.080 --> 00:43:56.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

